## Round 1.

? Steven Pinker recalls how he saw in this day in New Zealand. He quickly told the good news to his relatives using a phone, which was functioning properly. Identify this day.
! 1 January 2000.
= any notation of the same date.
/ In the late 20th century, the world was concerned about the so-called Y2K Bug, which meant that all computers might crash because they would not be able to identify the date. Steven Pinker saw in the year 2000 in New Zealand and was among the first people to make sure that everything was still working, so he could use technology to reassure his relatives in the US who still had to see the year in.
^ Стивен Пинкер. Просвещение продолжается. В защиту разума, науки, гуманизма и прогресса. // http://flibusta.site/b/637371/read (search string «так сложилось, что в судьбоносный момент»)
@ Alexander Karyasov

? In the mid-20th century, a special turkey breed was developed in the USA, which remained popular for several decades. This breed was adapted to fit the standard… what?
! oven.
= baking oven, bake oven.
/ With new, more convenient kitchen appliances, there emerged a demand for a smaller bird. Thus, a new breed was developed which could easily fit into an oven.
^ https://tellus.ars.usda.gov/stories/articles/changing-tastes-rise-and-fall-beltsville-small-white-turkey
@ Vladimir Sushkov

? [Handout:
.c..iv
]
A certain poem by Philip Larkin mentions grinning moustached faces, gardens left tidy, and marriages that will last only a little longer. In the title of this poem, which you see on the handout, some letters have been omitted. Write down the title in full.
! mcmxiv.
= MCMXIV
/ Larkin evokes images reminiscent of old photographs from the 1914 summer, when the First World War broke out. Soldiers leaving for the frontlines did not yet realize what lay ahead. The Roman numbers in the title refer to the importance of the year that changed Britain for good, as well as to the tradition of using Roman numbers on headstones.
^ 
- https://allpoetry.com/Mcmxiv
- https://poemanalysis.com/philip-larkin/mcmxiv/
@ Alexander Rozhdestvin

? In February 2017, the staff at the Atlanta Zoo consoled themselves by giving the name Tom to a cockroach. This happened BLANK. A TV show episode with the title Stress Relief set a viewership record because it aired BLANK. What three words does the BLANK stand for?
! after the Superbowl.
/ The Atlanta Falcons lost the 2017 Superbowl to Patriots spearheaded by Tom Brady, one of the most prominent personalities in American football. The zoo staff retaliated by naming a cockroach after him. An episode of The Office, titled Stress Relief, aired immediately after that year’s Superbowl and became the most watched episode of the series — it is likely that football fans relaxed and relieved their stress by watching it.
^ 
- https://firstandgoal.ru/2017/02/atlanta-zoo-names-cockroach-patriots-qb-tom-brady/
- https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Stress_Relief_(The_Office)
@ Alexander Karyasov

? During her election campaign, Alice Sachs ordered a batch of THESE at a restaurant. Identify THESE using only two words.
! fortune cookies.
/ Fortune cookies, with texts printed on slips of paper inside them, are popular in the US. However, it is unknown if such novelty advertisement helped Alice Sachs to succeed.
^ Паулина Брен. «Барбизон. В отеле только девушки» // http://flibusta.is/b/734691/read
@ Vladimir Sushkov

? The London poor lived in severely cramped homes. Henry Whitehead recalled that one such poor man lived in a THIS made of THAT in the middle of a shared room. In a well-known novella, a THIS made of THAT is not mentioned, and it is possible that in the film adaptation it was made of THAT for better visibility. Identify THIS made of THAT.
! circle made of chalk.
= chalk circle, circle drawn with chalk etc.
/ The poor man’s dwelling amounted to a chalk circle in the middle of a room. The corners were occupied by other tenants, even whole tenant families. In Gogol’s novella, Brut “draws a circle”, but it is not mentioned if it was drawn with chalk. In tales of magic, a circle works even if it is made with some other substance. However, for the film adaptation a jighly visible chalk circle was much more appropriate than one scraped with a knife or an imaginary one.
^ 
- Стивен Джонсон. Карта призраков. Как самая страшная эпидемия холеры в викторианском Лондоне изменила науку, города и современный мир // http://flibusta.is/b/589970/read
- https://ru.wikisource.org/wiki/Вий_(Гоголь)
- https://ru.wikipedia.org/wiki/Вий
@ Alexander Rozhdestvin

? Double  2 questions with 30 seconds’ conferring for each, the answers must be handed in on a single slip.
1. To visualize helplessness against the machinery of state, in a court scene of the film Crainquebille [cran-ker-BEEY] BLANKs are used for the main character. Fill the BLANK using only three words.
2. To visualize the feelings of power and awe inspired by Orson Welles’s character in the film Touch of Evil, BLANKs were frequently used for the actor. Fill the BLANK using only three words.
! 
- High(-)angle shots.
- Low(-)angle shots.
/ An appropriate choice of angle can evoke emotions. If someone is shot from a high angle, they will look smaller, more helpless. And, conversely, someone shot close up from a low angle looks menacing.
^ 
- https://ru.wikipedia.org/wiki/Кренкебиль_(фильм)
- https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Touch_of_Evil
@ Alexander Rozhdestvin

? Friedrich Parrot visited the village of Arghuri. He came to believe its name derived from the phrase “he planted a vine”. In 1829, Parrot became the first person in modern history… to do what?
! climb Ararat.
= conquer Ararat.
/ Friedrich Parrot headed the expedition that climbed the summit of Ararat in 1829, which became the first documented ascent. The village of Arghuri, the highest inhabited community on the slopes of Ararat, was his expedition’s starting point. Following the biblical legend, Parrot derived the name Arghuri from Armenian for “he planted a vine”, referring to the story of Noah planting vines after the flood.
^ 
- https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Friedrich_Parrot
- https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Yenidoğan,_Aralık
@ Alexander Rozhdestvin

? Philip Glass’s opera Akhenaten was commissioned by a specific theatre. The opera has a unique score because, unexpectedly to Glass, THIS turned out to be too small, and so he had to trim the score. Identify THIS.
! orchestra pit.
= pit.
/ Glass arrived at the theatre and found out that the orchestra he had written for would not fit into the pit. Eventually he had to cut the orchestra size in half and exclude the violins. This made the music gloomier.
^ Филип Гласс. «Слова без музыки. Воспоминания» // http://flibusta.is/b/720270/read
@ Vladimir Sushkov

? In the East, the principle behind THIS was described by Shen Kuo in the 11th century. In the West, it was done by Alexander Neckam a hundred years later. Identify THIS.
! compass.
/ The idea of compass travelled along a compass direction, from east to west. Shen Kuo was a Chinese polymath, Neckam was the abbot of an Augustinian monastery in Gloucestershire.
^ 
- https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Shen_Kuo
- https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Alexander_Neckam
- https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/History_of_the_compass
@ Alexander Rozhdestvin

? [Handout:
YIKES
]
The word “yikes” is shown to the user in case of connection issues. What is the user trying to do at that moment?
! play Wordle.
= equivalent answers, such as “solve a Worlde puzzle”, “visit the Wordle website” etc.
/ The word “yikes” has 5 letters, like all words playable in Wordle. That’s why the developers use it to indicate a connection problem.
