? Gardener Peter Collinson used to say that there was a specific way to obtain a kind of perpetuity. He himself obtained this kind of perpetuity, thanks to a certain person born in the village of Råshult [RAWS-hult]. Identify this person.
! [Carl] Linnaeus.
= [Carl von] Linné.
/ Collinson believed that if a biological species got its Latin name in honour of a person, this person obtained a kind of perpetuity. Collinson himself was honoured in this way by Linnaeus, who gave the name Collinsonia to a genus of plants.
^ Стивен Хёрд. «Усоногий рак Чарльза Дарвина и паук Дэвида Боуи. Как научные названия воспевают героев, авантюристов и негодяев» // http://flibusta.is/b/642332/read
@ Alexander Rozhdestvin

## Round 2

? Freda and Greta Chaplin wore identical clothes, walked in step, and even spoke simultaneously. Some said that Freda and Greta could sometimes make do without THIS. Identify THIS using only one word.
! mirror.
/ The twin sisters were identical and had all their actions synchronized to a high degree. They were even said to be able to dress themselves or arrange their hair by looking at each other.
^ https://www.yorkpress.co.uk/news/18791775.obituary-freda-chaplin-yorks-famous-twin-dies-aged-77/
@ Vladimir Sushkov

? The Barbizon Hotel had a rule that excluded men from the premises. The assistant manager recalled that every man trying to get inside had a certain object sticking out of their pocket. Identify this object using a word derived from Greek.
! stethoscope.
= phonendoscope.
/ There was an exception for doctors, so it was common for men to try and pass off as doctors. To make the trick more believable, they would display a clearly visible stethoscope in their pocket. However, the experienced manager could see through their ruse.
^ Паулина Брен. «Барбизон. В отеле только девушки» // http://flibusta.is/b/734691/read
@ Vladimir Sushkov

? [Note to the reader: do not mention the presence of an italicized title in the question text.]
In 1912, Akaki Tsereteli [tseh-reh-TEH-lee] visited the region of Racha-Lechkhumi [letch-KOO-mee]. It is believed that Akaki Tsereteli’s Voyage to Racha-Lechkhumi has a special importance in Georgia. Globally, the same importance is ascribed to the ending of another voyage which ended in a small town. This ending is known as… what?
! The Arrival of a Train.
= L'arrivée d'un train en gare de La Ciotat, The Arrival of a Train at La Ciotat Station, Arrival of a Train at La Ciotat, The Arrival of the Mail Train, Train Pulling into a Station.
/ Akaki’s voyage became the subject of an early documentary. This documentary is now believed to be the first film produced in Georgia. Globally, the first film ever produced is The Arrival of a Train by the Lumiere brothers.
^ 
- https://ru.wikipedia.org/wiki/Путешествие_Акакия_Церетели_в_Рача-Лечхуми
- https://www.ekhokavkaza.com/a/29325811.html
@ Alexander Rozhdestvin

? When Joanna of Flanders’s husband was thrown into prison, she headed his troops herself and took part in several battles. An admiring chronicler called Joanna a woman with THIS. Identify THIS.
! lion’s heart.
= lionheart, lioness’s heart.
/ Joanna was born about a hundred years after the death of another Lionheart, king Richard I. The war eventually ended in a victory for Joanna’s faction, but the contended ducal title went to her son, as her husband died during the war and she was imprisoned by the English king, who either believed she was insane or wanted to strengthen his own grip over Bretagne.
^ https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Joanna_of_Flanders
@ Alexander Rozhdestvin

? [Handout:
Unus Grex
]
The word grex is Latin for “flock”. When James I took the throne in both England and Scotland, he started to use a motto, one-third of which you can see on the handout. Write down this motto in full.
! Unus Rex, Unus Grex, Una Lex.
= Unus Rex, Unus Grex et Una Lex; Unus Rex, Unus Grex, Unus Lex; Unus Rex, Unus Grex and Unus Lex; one king, one flock/people, one law; any variant of the above with a different word order.
/ The motto meant “one king, one flock, one law”. The flock stood for his subjects, and even though this might sound condescending today, at the time it was a common metaphor. And it rhymed well.
^ https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Addled_Parliament
@ Alexander Rozhdestvin

? Currently, this country’s economy is quite healthy. The unemployment is only 3.6%, while the largest gas field is planned for closure in three years. Identify the country.
! The Netherlands.
= Holland.
/ The phrase “Dutch disease” means an economy that is overdependent on just one sector. The term was coined after Europe’s largest gas field was discovered in Groningen in mid-20th century. However, the Netherlands have coped with the “Dutch disease” — their economy has diversified by now, and the gas field that started it all is due for closure.
^ 
- https://ycharts.com/indicators/netherlands_unemployment_rate#:~:text=Netherlands Unemployment Rate is at,long term average of 5.76%
- https://www.forbes.ru/finansy/484040-niderlandy-resili-zakryt-krupnejsee-mestorozdenie-gaza-v-es-v-2023-godu
@ Alexander Karyasov

? Jenny Marx confessed guiltily that she sometimes wished her husband Karl HAD DONE THIS, because then she would have become indispensable for him. In a sense, Karl Marx DID THIS for real, after Jenny died. What is DOING THIS?
! losing his right hand.
/ Jenny’s train of thought: if Karl had lost his right hand, he would have grown to cherish and love his wife a lot more, because she would have been writing down his priceless thoughts. Figuratively, she was his right-hand person anyway, she helped and supported him all the time. Marx died 15 months after the death of his right-hand woman Jenny.
^ https://marxists.architexturez.net/archive/marx/letters/jenny/1839-jl1.htm
@ Alexander Rozhdestvin

? A certain journalist writes ruefully that there is now less money in consumer goods manufacturing because more money has been put into medical research. Steven Pinker refutes this and says that today one can have both THIS and THAT at the same time. Identify THIS or THAT.
! money.
= life.
/ According to Pinker, the fact that there is more money in drug development means that there are more funds available overall, which entails that modern societies can avoid choosing “their money or their life” and have both instead.
^ http://loveread.me/read_book.php?id=98192&p=137
@ Alexander Karyasov

? Brazil’s nature is enormously rich. Ian Urbina writes that it is not surprising that in the recent years one in every two environmentalists had been active in Brazil. What word has been omitted in the previous sentence?
! murdered.
= killed, assassinated.
/ Brazil is rich not only in natural diversity, but also in natural resources. These resources are the cause of many threats to, and severe destruction of, biodiversity, as well as a heavy toll on environmental activists — in recent years there have been about 50 murders of eco-activists in Brazil every year.
^ И. Урбина, «Океан вне закона. Работорговля, пиратство и контрабанда в нейтральных водах» // http://flibusta.is/b/634725/read
@ Alexander Rozhdestvin

? According to one interpretation, a certain English term from a popular game has nothing to do with water, but in fact derives from an Old French root meaning “to fix” or “to finalize”. What term is this?
! river.
/ According to one etymology, the term for the fifth open card on the poker table derives not from the English word river, but from the French verb river [ree-VAIR], meaning “fix” or “finalize”. (In English this root has also survived in the verb rivet.) And this makes sense because after the river the table is finalized.
^ https://www.quora.com/How-did-the-flop-turn-and-river-get-their-names-in-Texas-Holdem
@ Alexander Karyasov

? [Handout:
riso (Ital.) — rice
]
A dich which has “riso” [REE-zaw] in its name is prepared from several cuts of veal. Before serving, these cuts are brought together, and then they are served as one. What is the dish named after?
! risorgimento.
/ The dish comprises seven different cuts of veal that are brought together as it is being cooked. That is probably why it is named after the unification of Italy. There is no rice in it, just the letters riso.
^ https://www.terrerealidelpiemonte.it/en/tourist-accommodation/products/GRAN-BOLLITO-MISTO/
@ Alexander Karyasov

? Cynthia Bergstrom was inspired by the work of a certain European man when she was picking costume colours for a well-known slasher movie. Identify this European man.
! [Edvard] Munch.
/ The film is question is Scream. It is only a coincidence that it has the same name as Munch’s famous painting, but the painting did in fact inspire the costume designer of the film.
^ https://www.nylon.com/fashion/scream-movie-costume-designer-outfits-fashion
