? [Handout:
(img 2023-2024_II_en_001.png)]
These are hardtack biscuits used in the 19th century in the US army and navy. Army suppliers used KIKIs, navy suppliers used BOUBAs. What words are hidden behind KIKI and BOUBA?
! box, barrel.
= “crate” instead of “box”; also in reverse order.
/ The shape of hardtack was determined by storage conditions: The army used square-shaped boxes, the navy preferred round barrels, which were convenient in a ship.
^ https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Hardtack
@ Alexander Rozhdestvin

? A character in Earl Stanley Gardner’s books has a set of THESE for any necessity. For example, a spare THIS might be needed after a long drive or a sleepless night, when the difference is too obvious. Identify THESE.
! artificial eyes.
= glass eyes, eye prostheses, fake eyes etc.
/ The character explains that all his fake eyes look different. He might change them in the morning or in the evening, depending on how his healthy eye reacts to the light. After a long drive or a sleepless night his healthy eye might redden.
^ Эрл Стэнли Гарднер. «Дело о фальшивом глазе» // http://flibusta.is/b/165400/read
@ Vladimir Sushkov

? Paige Lambermont notes that the world is more complex than how it would seem in children’s stories. In an essay titled THIS MAN and the reason to DO THIS, written a few years ago, Lambermont gives specific advice — for example, Hanna Arendt’s book The Origin of Totalitarianism. What do THIS MAN and DO THIS stand for?
! [Harry] Potter, read another book.
= “read a second book”, “read a new book” instead of “read another book”.
/ Typically, people who are tired of hearing comparisons of real situations with the Harry Potter books just tell the other person to read another book. Paige Lambermont went a step further and gave specific recommendations for specific cases.
^ https://spectator.org/harry-potter-and-the-reason-to-read-another-book/
@ Alexander Karyasov

? In this question, the BLANK stands for a multiple-word phrase.
Judith Flanders writes that the earliest guild lists are dated with the year when the Earl of Leicester was liberated from captivity. Flanders believes that this odd date is explained by the fact that the people who made the list, and most people of the time, could not answer the following question: BLANK. Answer the question hidden behind the BLANK.
! 2023.
= twenty twenty-three.
/ The BLANK hides the question “Which year is it now?” Documents like the one mentioned here didn’t bear a specific date, but they might have referred to a notable event to have at least some time indication. For example, this specific guild list was dated with “the year when the Earl of Leicester was liberated from captivity”. Judith Flanders believes that the makers of these lists really did not know what year it was and used such references out of necessity.
^ Джудит Фландерс. Всему свое место. Необыкновенная история алфавитного порядка. // https://bit.ly/45AKZK7
@ Alexander Karyasov

